From: Tim Douglas <me@timdoug.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Start the shell and helpers with vfork

Each child works on its own copies and then execs or _exits, never
returning, so vfork does, and it is the only way to start a process on
a machine without an MMU.  The helper child's setenv() calls reach the
parent's environment too, which only those launches read.

Signed-off-by: Tim Douglas <me@timdoug.com>
Upstream: not submitted

--- a/slave.c
+++ b/slave.c
@@ -382,7 +382,11 @@
         return -1;
     }
 
-    pid_t pid = fork();
+    /* The child works on its own copies (environment, signals, directory,
+     * terminal) and then execs or _exits, never returning from here, so
+     * vfork does: it is all a machine without an MMU has.  What it
+     * allocates stays allocated in the parent. */
+    pid_t pid = vfork();
     switch (pid) {
     case -1:
         LOG_ERRNO("failed to fork");
--- a/spawn.c
+++ b/spawn.c
@@ -26,7 +26,10 @@
         goto err;
     }
 
-    pid_t pid = fork();
+    /* vfork, as for the shell: the child execs or _exits.  Its setenv()
+     * calls also change the parent's environment, which only these
+     * helper launches read. */
+    pid_t pid = vfork();
     if (pid < 0) {
         LOG_ERRNO("failed to fork");
         goto err;
